---
import Note from '../../../layouts/Note.astro';
---
<Note ch={3} description="Why attention cannot see word order, and how learned absolute position embeddings fix it, with their limits.">
  <header class="hero">
    <div>
      <p class="kicker">chapter 03</p>
      <h1><span>Positional information</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">Attention compares every token with every other token, and it does not care where they sit.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="say intro"><p>Here's a strange fact about attention, the core part of a transformer. On its own, it has no idea what order the words came in. It looks at the whole sentence like a bag of words. "Dog bites man" and "man bites dog" would look identical to it, and those obviously mean very different things.</p><p>So we need to tell the model where each token sits. This chapter shows the problem, then the simplest fix, which GPT-2 used: learn a vector for every position and add it to the token's vector. The next two chapters cover smarter fixes.</p></div>

  <h2>The blind spot</h2>
  <div class="say"><p>First, let's see the problem with our own eyes, then pin down what "position" even means.</p></div>

  <figure class="fig" id="shuffle">
    <div class="fig-head"><h3>The shuffle test</h3></div>
    <figcaption class="say"><p>Two sentences with the same three words in a different order. Each strip shows what attention outputs for that word. With "Add positions" off, "dog" gets the exact same strip in both sentences. Attention can't tell who bit whom.</p><p>Flip the toggle on. Now the strips differ between the sentences, because each word also carries information about where it sits. That difference is all we're trying to add in this chapter and the next two.</p></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure class="fig" id="seats">
    <div class="fig-head"><h3>Two kinds of “where”</h3></div>
    <figcaption class="say"><p>There are two ways to describe a position. <b>Absolute</b> position is "I'm in seat 5", a fixed number counted from the start. <b>Relative</b> position is "that word is two seats behind me", which only cares about the gap between two tokens.</p><p>For language, relative position is usually what matters. "The" right before "cat" means the same thing whether it's at the start of a book or on page 300. Keep this in mind, because it's the reason later methods like RoPE won.</p></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Learned absolute positions</h2>
  <div class="say"><p>The simplest fix borrows the idea from chapter 2. Make a second table, this time with one row per position instead of one per token. Look up the row for each position and add it to the token's vector. Now "dog at position 0" and "dog at position 2" get different inputs.</p></div>

  <figure class="fig" id="add">
    <div class="fig-head"><h3>Token vector + position vector</h3></div>
    <div class="formula">x<sub>i</sub> = <em>E[id<sub>i</sub>]</em> + <em>P[i]</em></div>
    <figcaption class="say"><p>Pick a token and a position with the sliders. The top strip is the token's vector from the embedding table. The middle strip is the position's vector from the position table. The bottom strip is the two added together, number by number. That sum is what goes into the first layer.</p><p>Adding is a bit of a squeeze. The same numbers now carry both "what word" and "where". The model learns to keep the two apart well enough, but it's not a clean separation.</p></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure class="fig" id="ptable">
    <div class="fig-head"><h3>The position table</h3></div>
    <figcaption class="say"><p>Press play. This is the position table, drawn as a heatmap with one row per position and colour showing the values. As the animation runs, each token of our sentence fetches the row for its position, 0, 1, 2 and so on. Notice the table doesn't care which word it is. Position 3 gets row 3, no matter what's there.</p></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure class="fig" id="psim">
    <div class="fig-head"><h3>What the learned table ends up looking like</h3></div>
    <figcaption class="say"><p>After training, what do these position vectors look like? This grid compares every position with every other one. The bright diagonal says each position is most similar to itself, which is expected. More interesting, the brightness fades smoothly as you move away from the diagonal. Position 8 is quite similar to 7 and 9, and less similar to 2 or 14.</p><p>Nobody asked for that. The model learned on its own that nearby positions should look alike. It's discovering a kind of relative position, even though we only gave it absolute ones.</p></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Where learned positions break</h2>
  <div class="say"><p>This works, and GPT-2 shipped with it. But it has two real weaknesses, which is why newer models moved on.</p></div>

  <figure class="fig" id="wall">
    <div class="fig-head"><h3>The maximum-length wall</h3></div>
    <figcaption class="say"><p>Drag the sequence length slider past 12. The table only has rows for positions 0 to 11, because that's the longest text the model saw in training. Tokens past that have no row to fetch, shown by the red crosses.</p><p>This is a hard limit. A model trained with 1,024 positions literally cannot read token 1,025 unless you add and train new rows. GPT-2 had exactly this wall at 1,024 tokens.</p></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure class="fig" id="shift">
    <div class="fig-head"><h3>Same relation, different numbers</h3></div>
    <figcaption class="say"><p>The same pair of words, "the cat", appears twice, once at positions 2 and 3, once at positions 102 and 103. The gap between them is one step both times, so the relationship is the same. But because each position has its own unrelated vector, the inputs the model sees are completely different.</p><p>So the model has to learn "the then cat" separately at every place it could appear. That's wasted effort, and it's the main reason people looked for a relative scheme.</p></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure class="fig" id="where">
    <div class="fig-head"><h3>Three answers, three injection points</h3></div>
    <figcaption class="say"><p>Three ways to give the model position information, and where each one plugs in. <b>Learned</b> positions (this chapter) and <b>sinusoidal</b> positions (next chapter) are both added to the token vectors once, at the very bottom, before the first layer. <b>RoPE</b> (chapter 5) does something different. It doesn't touch the input at all. It acts inside every attention layer, on the queries and keys. Don't worry if those words are new, they come in chapter 6.</p></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Exceptions and sharp edges</h2>
  <div class="say"><p>Things that go wrong with positions in practice.</p></div>
  <div class="edges">
    <article class="edge redraw" id="e-pad"><div class="art"></div><h4>Left padding shifts positions</h4><p>When you pad short prompts on the left so they line up on the right, the real first word ends up at position 3 or 4 instead of 0. The model then thinks the text started later than it did.</p><span class="fix">compute position ids from the attention mask, not from the raw index.</span></article>
    <article class="edge redraw" id="e-pack"><div class="art"></div><h4>Packed documents</h4><p>To save space, training often packs several short documents into one long row. Should the second document's positions restart at 0, or carry on from the first? Both work, but the masks have to agree with your choice.</p><span class="fix">pick one, and use matching attention masks.</span></article>
    <article class="edge redraw" id="e-tail"><div class="art"></div><h4>Under-trained tail rows</h4><p>If most training texts are short, the rows for high positions barely get trained. The model then behaves worse near the end of long inputs, even inside the official limit.</p><span class="fix">include enough long examples, or use a scheme without a table.</span></article>
    <article class="edge redraw" id="e-nope"><div class="art"></div><h4>No positions is not zero information</h4><p>Oddly, a model with causal masking (chapter 8) and no position information at all can still work out some order, because each token sees a different number of earlier tokens. Interesting, but much weaker than real positions.</p><span class="fix">a curiosity, not a replacement for real positions.</span></article>
    <article class="edge redraw" id="e-swap"><div class="art"></div><h4>Swapping position scheme</h4><p>Position schemes are baked into the trained weights. You can't take a model trained with learned positions and switch it to RoPE without a lot of retraining.</p><span class="fix">decide at the start, or plan a fine-tuning phase.</span></article>
  </div>

  <Fragment slot="scripts"><script is:inline>
const { fig, box, rect, o, arr, carr, ln, node, chip, heat, vec, bars, axes, smooth, cross, check, squig, clamp, map, gauss, rand, cos, dot, softmax, matmul } = Ink;
const D = 6;
const emb = { dog: [0.9, -0.2, 0.4, 0.1, -0.6, 0.3], bites: [-0.3, 0.8, 0.1, -0.5, 0.2, 0.6], man: [0.4, 0.3, -0.7, 0.6, 0.1, -0.2], the: [0.1, -0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 0.1], cat: [0.8, 0.1, 0.5, -0.2, -0.4, 0.4], sat: [-0.2, 0.6, 0.3, -0.4, 0.4, 0.5], it: [0.6, 0, 0.4, 0.2, -0.3, 0.2] };
const Pm = Array.from({ length: 16 }, (_, i) => Array.from({ length: D }, (_, j) => +(0.7 * Math.sin(i * (0.25 + j * 0.13) + j * 1.7) + gauss(i + 50, j) * 0.12).toFixed(2)));
const attn = X => X.map(q => { const w = softmax(X.map(k => dot(q, k))); return q.map((_, d) => w.reduce((s, wi, j) => s + wi * X[j][d], 0)); });
const same = (a, b) => a.every((v, i) => Math.abs(v - b[i]) < 1e-9);

fig('shuffle', {
  view: '0 0 960 400', hue: 195, state: { pos: false },
  label: 'Two sentences, dog bites man and man bites dog, with each token attention output drawn as a colour strip. Without positions the strips for each word match across sentences. With positions they differ.',
  draw(sk, st) {
    const A = ['dog', 'bites', 'man'], B = ['man', 'bites', 'dog'];
    const X = s => s.map((w, i) => emb[w].map((v, d) => v + (st.pos ? Pm[i][d] * 1.2 : 0)));
    const oA = attn(X(A)), oB = attn(X(B));
    const draw = (s, out, y, lab) => {
      sk.hand(70, y + 24, lab, 20, -3);
      s.forEach((w, i) => {
        const x = 160 + i * 270;
        chip(sk, x, y, 80, 32, w, { hi: w === 'dog', s: 13, sub: st.pos ? 'pos ' + i : null });
        vec(sk, out[i], x - 20, y + 52, 20, 22, { max: 1.2 });
      });
    };
    draw(A, oA, 40, 'sentence A');
    draw(B, oB, 250, 'sentence B');
    ['dog', 'man', 'bites'].forEach(w => {
      const i = A.indexOf(w), j = B.indexOf(w), eq = same(oA[i], oB[j]);
      const x1 = 200 + i * 270, x2 = 200 + j * 270;
      (eq ? sk.ink.bind(sk) : sk.red.bind(sk))(ln(x1, 126, x2, 238, i));
      sk.hand((x1 + x2) / 2 + (i === j ? 22 : 0), 188, eq ? '=' : '≠', 30, 0, { c: eq ? '' : 'redt' });
    });
    sk.hand(820, 380, st.pos ? 'positions make order matter' : 'identical outputs, just reordered', 21, -2, { c: st.pos ? 'tint' : 'redt' });
  },
  controls: c => c.toggle('pos', 'Add positions').note('dog has the accent')
});

fig('seats', {
  view: '0 0 960 280', hue: 195,
  label: 'A row of nine theatre seats numbered 0 to 8. Seat 5 is highlighted with the label I am in seat 5. A curved arrow from seat 3 to seat 5 is labelled two seats behind me.',
  draw(sk) {
    for (let i = 0; i < 9; i++) {
      const x = 80 + i * 92;
      const d = `M${x} 150 ${x + 64} 148 ${x + 66} 196 ${x - 2} 198Z`;
      i === 5 ? sk.accent(d) : sk.paper(d); sk.ink(d);
      sk.ink(`M${x + 4} 150C${x + 6} 120 ${x + 58} 118 ${x + 62} 149`);
      sk.ink(`M${x + 6} 198v20M${x + 58} 197v21`);
      sk.mono(x + 32, 180, String(i), { s: 14 });
    }
    sk.hand(572, 92, 'absolute: I am in seat 5', 22, -4);
    sk.ink(arr(570, 100, 572, 130));
    sk.pop(carr(298, 232, 534, 232, -40));
    sk.hand(420, 272, 'relative: you are 2 seats behind me', 21, -1, { c: 'tint' });
  }
});

const toks = Object.keys(emb);
fig('add', {
  view: '0 0 960 300', hue: 195, state: { t: 1, p: 2 },
  label: 'Three colour strips: the token vector, the position vector, and their sum, with values written in the cells.',
  draw(sk, st) {
    const e = emb[toks[st.t]], p = Pm[st.p], x = e.map((v, i) => +(v + p[i]).toFixed(2));
    vec(sk, e, 260, 40, 70, 44, { nums: true, max: 1.6, label: `E["${toks[st.t]}"]`, labS: 14 });
    sk.hand(470, 118, '+', 34);
    vec(sk, p, 260, 128, 70, 44, { nums: true, max: 1.6, label: `P[${st.p}]`, labS: 14 });
    sk.ink('M240 194H700');
    vec(sk, x, 260, 210, 70, 44, { nums: true, max: 1.6, label: 'x', labS: 16 });
    sk.pop(box(254, 204, 432, 56, 3));
    sk.hand(840, 120, `“${toks[st.t]}” in seat ${st.p}`, 22, -4, { c: 'tint' });
  },
  controls: c => c.slider('t', 'Token', 0, toks.length - 1, 1, v => toks[v]).slider('p', 'Position', 0, 15, 1)
});

fig('ptable', {
  view: '0 0 960 360', hue: 195,
  label: 'A heatmap of the position table P with 16 rows and 6 columns. Rows 0 to 6 are fetched one by one for the seven tokens of the running sentence.',
  draw(sk, st) {
    const words = ['The', '␠cat', '␠sat', '␠because', '␠it', '␠was', '␠tired'];
    words.forEach((w, i) => chip(sk, 30, 30 + i * 44, 100, 32, w, { hi: i === st.step, dim: i > st.step, s: 12, }));
    heat(sk, Pm, 330, 30, 40, 19, { max: 1, rows: Pm.map((_, i) => String(i)), hiRow: st.step });
    sk.hand(450, 352, 'P   [L_max × d]', 20, -2);
    sk.ink(carr(134, 46 + st.step * 44, 300, 40 + st.step * 19, -14));
    sk.ink(arr(580, 40 + st.step * 19, 640, 40 + st.step * 19));
    vec(sk, Pm[st.step], 650, 26 + st.step * 19, 44, 28, { nums: true, max: 1 });
    sk.hand(790, 300, 'rows 7–15 unused today', 19, -3, { c: 'muted' });
  },
  controls: c => c.player('step', 7, { ms: 900, name: 'position' })
});

fig('psim', {
  view: '0 0 960 360', hue: 195,
  label: 'A 16 by 16 cosine similarity matrix between learned position rows, bright along the diagonal and fading away from it. A side chart shows similarity of position 8 to all others.',
  draw(sk) {
    const L = 16, P = Array.from({ length: L }, (_, i) => Array.from({ length: 24 }, (_, j) => Math.sin(i * (0.08 + j * 0.03) + j) + gauss(i + 9, j) * 0.15));
    const M = P.map(a => P.map(b => cos(a, b)));
    heat(sk, M, 40, 30, 19, 19, { max: 1, grid: false, neg: true });
    sk.mono(192, 350, 'position j →', { s: 11 }); sk.mono(28, 190, 'i', { s: 11 });
    axes(sk, 440, 40, 460, 240, { xl: 'position j →', yl: 'cos(P[8], P[j])' });
    const y = v => map(v, -0.4, 1, 280, 40);
    const pts = M[8].map((v, j) => [460 + j * 28, y(v)]);
    sk.plot(smooth(pts), 'pop');
    pts.forEach(([x, yy], j) => { sk.paper(o(x, yy, 4)); sk.ink(o(x, yy, 4)); });
    sk.dash(`M${460 + 8 * 28} 40V280`);
    sk.hand(700, 70, 'neighbours look alike', 21, -3, { c: 'tint' });
  }
});

fig('wall', {
  view: '0 0 960 260', hue: 195, state: { T: 9 },
  label: 'Input tokens laid out in a row with the learned position table below covering positions 0 to 11. Tokens beyond position 11 have no table row and are marked with red crosses.',
  draw(sk, st) {
    const Lmax = 12, w = 44;
    for (let i = 0; i < 20; i++) {
      const x = 30 + i * 46;
      if (i < st.T) chip(sk, x, 50, w - 4, 32, 'tok', { s: 10, red: i >= Lmax, hi: i === st.T - 1 && i < Lmax });
      if (i < Lmax) { sk.cell(rect(x, 130, w - 4, 34), 0.25 + 0.5 * Math.abs(Math.sin(i))); sk.ink(box(x, 130, w - 4, 34, i)); sk.mono(x + 20, 151, String(i), { s: 11 }); }
      if (i < st.T && i < Lmax) sk.thin(`M${x + 20} 86V126`);
      if (i < st.T && i >= Lmax) sk.red(cross(x + 20, 150, 10));
    }
    sk.red(`M${30 + Lmax * 46 - 4} 30V200`);
    sk.hand(30 + Lmax * 46 + 6, 228, 'end of the table', 20, -3, { a: 'start', c: 'redt' });
    sk.hand(140, 228, 'P has 12 rows', 20, -2);
    if (st.T > Lmax) sk.raw(`<foreignObject x="640" y="20" width="300" height="60"><div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="text-align:right"><span class="stamp slam">IndexError: 12</span></div></foreignObject>`);
  },
  controls: c => c.slider('T', 'Sequence length', 1, 20, 1)
});

fig('shift', {
  view: '0 0 960 320', hue: 195,
  label: 'The pair the cat drawn twice: at positions 2 and 3, and at positions 102 and 103. The summed input vectors differ in each case even though the distance is 1 in both.',
  draw(sk) {
    const P = i => Array.from({ length: D }, (_, j) => 0.8 * Math.sin(i * (0.4 + j * 0.37) + j * 2.1));
    const sum = (w, i) => emb[w].map((v, j) => v + P(i)[j]);
    [[2, 40], [102, 500]].forEach(([p, x], k) => {
      chip(sk, x + 40, 40, 70, 30, 'the', { s: 12, sub: 'pos ' + p });
      chip(sk, x + 230, 40, 70, 30, 'cat', { s: 12, hi: true, sub: 'pos ' + (p + 1) });
      sk.pop(carr(x + 110, 54, x + 228, 54, -24));
      sk.mono(x + 170, 28, 'distance 1', { s: 11 });
      vec(sk, sum('the', p), x + 20, 140, 18, 24, { max: 1.6 });
      vec(sk, sum('cat', p + 1), x + 210, 140, 18, 24, { max: 1.6 });
      sk.thin(`M${x + 75} 96V136M${x + 265} 96V136`);
    });
    sk.hand(480, 240, 'same grammar, completely different inputs', 22, -2, { c: 'redt' });
    sk.ink('M440 120V200');
  }
});

fig('where', {
  view: '0 0 960 340', hue: 195,
  label: 'Three small model diagrams. Learned and sinusoidal: position vectors added to the token embeddings before the first block. RoPE: queries and keys rotated inside every attention layer.',
  draw(sk) {
    [['learned', 160, 'add'], ['sinusoidal', 480, 'add'], ['RoPE', 800, 'rot']].forEach(([t, cx, kind]) => {
      sk.hand(cx, 36, t, 24, -3);
      node(sk, cx - 70, 250, 140, 40, 'token emb', { font: 'mono', s: 12 });
      node(sk, cx - 70, 150, 140, 60, '', { fill: 'paper' });
      node(sk, cx - 70, 70, 140, 50, '', { fill: 'paper' });
      sk.mono(cx, 186, 'attention', { s: 12 }); sk.mono(cx, 100, 'attention', { s: 12 });
      sk.ink(arr(cx, 248, cx, 214)); sk.ink(arr(cx, 148, cx, 124));
      if (kind === 'add') { sk.accent(o(cx + 100, 230, 14)); sk.ink(o(cx + 100, 230, 14)); sk.hand(cx + 100, 237, '+', 22); sk.ink(arr(cx + 86, 230, cx + 6, 230)); sk.mono(cx + 100, 262, kind === 'add' && t === 'learned' ? 'P[i]' : 'PE(i)', { s: 11 }); }
      else { [168, 84].forEach(y => { sk.accent(o(cx + 48, y + 8, 10)); sk.ink(o(cx + 48, y + 8, 10)); sk.ink(`M${cx + 41} ${y + 2}A9 9 0 1 1 ${cx + 44} ${y + 17}`); }); sk.mono(cx + 92, 140, 'rotate q, k', { s: 11 }); }
    });
    sk.hand(320, 318, 'once, at the door', 20, -2, { c: 'tint' }); sk.hand(800, 318, 'in every layer', 20, -2, { c: 'tint' });
  }
});

const Ed = { view: '0 0 240 110', hue: 195 };
fig('e-pad', { ...Ed, draw(sk) { ['pad', 'pad', 'pad', 'hi', 'you'].forEach((t, i) => chip(sk, 12 + i * 44, 30, 40, 28, t, { dim: i < 3, hi: i === 3, s: 10, sub: String(i) })); sk.hand(160, 104, 'hi is at 3?!', 17, -3, { c: 'redt' }); } });
fig('e-pack', { ...Ed, draw(sk) { for (let i = 0; i < 8; i++) chip(sk, 14 + i * 27, 32, 24, 28, i < 4 ? 'A' : 'B', { hi: i >= 4, s: 11, sub: String(i < 4 ? i : `${i - 4}?`) }); sk.red('M122 20V72'); } });
fig('e-tail', { ...Ed, draw(sk) { bars(sk, [60, 50, 40, 28, 18, 9, 4, 1], 30, 16, 180, 64, { hiSet: new Set([6, 7]) }); sk.mono(120, 100, 'updates per position row', { s: 10 }); } });
fig('e-nope', { ...Ed, draw(sk) { heat(sk, [[1, -Infinity, -Infinity, -Infinity], [1, 1, -Infinity, -Infinity], [1, 1, 1, -Infinity], [1, 1, 1, 1]], 60, 12, 20, 20, { max: 1, masked: false }); ['1', '2', '3', '4'].forEach((t, i) => sk.mono(154, 26 + i * 20, `sees ${t}`, { s: 10, a: 'start' })); } });
fig('e-swap', { ...Ed, draw(sk) { node(sk, 20, 34, 80, 36, 'learned', { font: 'mono', s: 11 }); node(sk, 140, 34, 80, 36, 'RoPE', { font: 'mono', s: 11, fill: 'accent' }); sk.ink(arr(104, 52, 136, 52)); sk.red(cross(120, 52, 9)); } });
  </script></Fragment>
</Note>
